{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfbJUatgx8S5ApeRCKTbE", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The main difference is that a list is mutable and a tuple is immutable.\n\n| | **List** | **Tuple** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` or `1, 2, 3` |\n| Mutability | Can be changed after creation | Cannot be changed after creation |\n| Methods | `append`, `remove`, `sort`, and more | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its elements are hashable |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly larger and slower | Slightly smaller and faster |\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # works\nnums.append(4)        # works\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\n**When to use which**\n- **List:** Use it for a collection that grows, shrinks, or is reordered, usually with items of the same kind (a list of users, for example).\n- **Tuple:** Use it for a fixed record where position carries meaning, such as `(x, y)` coordinates or `(name, age)`. Tuples are also what you get when a function returns multiple values, and you can use them as dict keys.\n\n**Caveats**\n- A tuple is only shallowly immutable. `([1, 2], 3)` still lets you do `t[0].append(9)`, because the list inside is mutable.\n- A one-element tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`. Plain `(1)` is just the integer `1`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 6686, "cache_read_input_tokens": 11903, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 6686}, "output_tokens": 6, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "f